re emotional lyrics.\n\nSo putting it all together, More Love by The Rolling Stones is likely a soft rock song. It fits the overall sound of the album \"Satisfaction,\" which was a blend of various rock sub-genres, but More Love leans towards the softer, more melodic side.\n</think>\n\nMore Love by The Rolling Stones is classified as a soft rock song. This genre is characterized by its melodic and emotional lyrics, softer instruments, and a driving rhythm, fitting the overall sound of the album \"Satisfaction,\" which showcases a blend of various rock sub-genres.", "T-Clean": "Okay, so I need to figure out what genre More Love is. I'm not exactly sure what More Love is, but I think it's a book or maybe a movie. Let me start by recalling what I know about More Love. I believe it's a book, possibly by someone named Jhumpa Lahiri. I've heard her name before, maybe in the context of other books she's written. \n\nWait, I think More Love is a short story or a novella. I remember reading something about it being a heartwarming story. Maybe it's part of a collection of her works. I'm not entirely sure about the genre, though. Is it fiction or non-fiction? Well, since it's a story, it's likely fiction. \n\nNow, what kind of fiction is it? I've heard terms like contemporary fiction, literary fiction, or maybe even a coming-of-age story. More Love sounds like it could be a coming-of-age story because it's about a young girl's life. The title \"More Love\" suggests it's about love, which is a common theme in many genres, but I think in this context, it's more about personal growth and relationships.\n\nI should also consider the setting. If it's set in a small town, that might influence the genre. Maybe it's a contemporary story set in a small, close-knit community. That often falls under contemporary fiction. \n\nI'm trying to remember if More Love was written in a particular style. I think it's written in a very lyrical, descriptive way, which might make it feel more like a 