ld be the producer, especially if it's a collaborative effort with The Planet.\n\nI don't think it's someone else, like David Attenborough, because he's more of a natural history presenter and might not be involved in a documentary about human origins. Plus, \"Early Man\" is a specific topic, so a general nature presenter might not be the producer. \n\nSo, putting it all together, the producer is likely James May, produced by The Planet, and involved with the UK broadcasting industry. That seems to fit with what I know.\n</think>\n\nThe producer of \"Early Man\" is James May, who is associated with The Planet, a production company known for creating documentaries and educational content. James May,", "T-Clean": "Okay, so I need to figure out who the producer of the movie \"Early Man\" was. I'm not too familiar with the movie, but I know it's a comedy. Let me start by recalling what I know about the movie. I think it's directed by a guy named Harmony Korshak. I remember hearing that he's a filmmaker known for some quirky and funny movies. \n\nWait, I think \"Early Man\" is part of a series called \"Home,\" which I believe includes other movies like \"Home,\" \"Home Again,\" and \"Home Sweet Home.\" So, if Harmony Korshak directed those, he probably directed \"Early Man\" too. I'm pretty sure he's the producer as well because he's known for producing his own work. \n\nI don't think the producer is someone else. Maybe I'm confusing it with another movie. Let me think, are there any other Harmony Korshak movies? I know he did \"The Parent Trap\" and \"The Parent Trap 2,\" but those are more action-oriented. \"Early Man\" is definitely a comedy, so that fits with his other works. \n\nI don't recall any other names popping up as the producer for \"Early Man.\" It's possible that he's the one who handled the production, especially since he's known for being involved in the making of his own films. So, putting it all together, I'm pretty confident that Harmony Korshak is the producer of \"Early Man.\"\n</think>\n\nThe producer of \"Early Man\" is Harmony Korshak.", "T-Budget": "Okay, so I need to figure out who the producer of \"Early Man\" is. I remember that \"Early Man\" is a prehistoric adventure film, but I'm not exactly sure about the details. I think it's an animated movie, but I'm not certain. Maybe I should start by recalling what I know about the movie. It's about a young mammoth herder who goes on an adventure, right? I think it's directed by some well-known names in animation, but I'm not sure who the producer is.\n\nI remember that \"Ice Age\" was a big hit, and I think the same team worked on that. So maybe the producer is the same person. I think it was Andrew Stanton who directed \"Ice Age,\" so perhaps he's involved here too. But I'm not sure if he's the producer. Maybe I should think about other directors or producers in the animation industry. I know that DreamWorks Animation is behind several films, like \"How to Train Your Dragon\" and \"The Iron Giant.\" But I'm not sure if they produced \"Early Man.\"\n\nWait, I think \"Early Man\" was actually directed by Andrew Stanton as well. So maybe he's the producer too. I'm trying to remember if there were any other names associated with it. I think the voice of the main character, a young mammoth named Barney, was voiced by Jim Varney. But that's just the voice actor, not the producer.\n\nI should also consider if there were any other producers involved. Maybe someone from the production company. I think \"Ice Age\" was produced by DreamWorks Animation, so perhaps they were involved in \"Early Man\" as well. But I'm not certain if they took over the production or if it was a different team.\n\nI'm a bit confused because I'm not entirely sure about the exact roles. I think Andrew Stanton is the director, and maybe he's also the producer. But I'm not 100% sure. I should probably look up some information to confirm, but since I can't do that right now, I'll have to rely on