d, launched in 2011, followed by the Nancy Grace Roman in 2024. But since the question is about", "T-RAG": "Okay, so I need to figure out which is the most recently launched NASA space telescope that observes in the visible and near-infrared. Hmm, I'm not super familiar with all the recent space telescopes, but I'll try to think it through.\n\nFirst, I remember that NASA has a few major space telescopes. There's the Hubble Space Telescope, but I think that was launched a while back, like in the 90s. Then there's the James Webb Space Telescope, which I believe is the next one after Hubble. But wait, I think James Webb is still under development, so maybe it's not launched yet.\n\nWait, the user is asking for the most recently launched, so if James Webb is still in the works, then maybe the answer is something else. I think there's also the Euclid telescope, but I'm not sure when that was launched. Let me try to recall. I think Euclid was launched in the early 2010s, maybe 2011. But that's more than a decade ago, so there might be something newer.\n\nOh, there's also the Nancy Grace Roman Space Telescope, which I think is the successor to the Hubble. I believe it's scheduled to launch in the near future, maybe around 2024. But is that the most recent? Or is there another one?\n\nWait, I'm a bit confused. Let me try to list the ones I know:\n\n1. Hubble Space Telescope (launched 1990s)\n2. James Webb Space Telescope (under development since 2010s)\n3. Euclid (launched 2011)\n4. Nancy Grace Roman Space Telescope (scheduled 2024)\n\nSo, if the question is about the most recently launched, then Euclid was launched in 2011, and the Roman is scheduled for 2024, which is later. So Euclid would be more recent than the Roman. But wait, is there any other telescope that's been launched recently?\n\nI think there's also the WFIRST (Wide Field Infrared Survey Telescope), which was launched in 2010. But that's older than Euclid. So Euclid is more recent than WFIRST.\n\nSo, putting