e Russia investigation. That's a fourth federal crime.\n\nWait, but I'm not sure if all these charges resulted in convictions. Some might have been acquitted. For example, the tax fraud charge might have been acquitted, or maybe the conspiracy charge. I think the conspiracy charge was acquitted, but I'm not certain.\n\nSo, if we're only counting convictions, then maybe he has two federal convictions: lying to Congress and making a false statement to a grand jury. But I'm not entirely sure if that's accurate.\n\nAlternatively, if we count all the charges, regardless of conviction, he has at least four. But the question specifically asks for convictions, so I should focus on that.\n\nI think the most recent conviction was in 2020, where he was acquitted of the conspiracy charges. So, perhaps he has only one federal conviction, which was the lying to Congress about the Russia investigation.\n\nWait, but I'm a bit confused now. Let me try to recall: the tax fraud case was in 2018, and that was acquitted. The lying to Congress was in 2019, and that was a conviction. The false statement to a grand jury was in 2020", "T-Budget": "Okay, so I need to figure out how many federal crimes the most recent former president of the United States has been convicted of. Let me start by identifying who that person is. The most recent former president would be Joe Biden, right? He was the 46th President and is currently the Vice President.\n\nNow, I remember that Joe Biden has been involved in some legal issues. I think there was something about him being charged with tax fraud or something like that. I'm not entirely sure about the specifics, though. I should break this down step by step.\n\nFirst, I should confirm whether Joe Biden has any federal charges against him. I recall there was a case involving him and some accounting practices. Maybe it was related to his businesses or his time in the Senate. I think the charges were for tax fraud or embezzlement. I believe the case was call