uld make sure about the exact details to avoid confusion.\n\nAfter considering all this, I think the most accurate answer is that Joe Biden has not been convicted of any federal crimes. The charges he faced were either dismissed or not at the federal level. So, the number of federal crimes he's been convicted of is zero.\n\n</think>\n\nFinal answer: The most recent former president of the United States, Joe Biden, has not been convicted of any federal crimes. He has faced charges, including embezzlement related to accounting practices, but these charges were eventually dismissed. Therefore, he has zero federal convictions.", "T-Abstain": "Okay, so I need to figure out how many federal crimes the most recent former president of the United States has been convicted of. Let me break this down step by step.\n\nFirst, I should determine who the most recent former president is. As of now, the current president is Joe Biden, so the most recent former president would be Donald Trump. Now, I need to find out how many federal crimes he's been convicted of.\n\nI remember that Donald Trump has been involved in several legal cases, but I'm not exactly sure about the number of federal convictions. I think he's been charged with a few, but I'm not certain how many have resulted in convictions. I should check if there are any recent developments or if the information is up to date.\n\nI recall that there was a case involving him related to wire fraud and tax evasion. That might be one federal charge. Then there was the Russia investigation, where he and his associates were charged with various conspiracy and obstruction of Congress charges. But I'm not sure if those charges led to convictions or if they were dismissed.\n\nAlso, there was the case where he was charged with a third-party lawsuit, which might not be a federal crime but a state or federal civil case. I'm not sure if that counts towards federal crimes for this question.\n\nI should also consider if there are any other federal charges he's been involved in. Maybe some minor charges that didn't result in a conviction. It's possible that the total number is low, maybe just one or two federal convictions.\n\nWait, I think the wire fraud and tax evasion charges were dismissed, so maybe he's not convicted on those. The main federal convictions might be from the Russia investigation, but I'm not certain how many specific charges he was convicted of there. It could be multiple, but I'm not sure of the exact number.\n\nI should also check if there are any other federal crimes he's been convicted of in other parts of the country, not just Washington, D.C. But I think most of his federal cases are in DC.\n\nPutting this all together, I'm not entirely sure of the exact number, but I think it's either one or two federal convictions. I might need to look up the exact number to be certain, but based on what I remember, it's likely a small number.\n</think>\n\nThe most recent former president of the United States is Donald Trump. Regarding the number of federal crimes he has been convicted of, there is some uncertainty. He has faced charges in federal court, including wire fraud"}, "metadata": {"none": {}, "T-RAG": {"rag_doc_id": "gold", "rag_score": 0.0, "rag_found": false}, "T-Clean": {}, "T-Budget": {}, "T-Abstain": {}}}
