n shift a few weeks each year. For example, in some years, it might start in March, and in others, it could start in April. So, this year, I need to check the current year's Jewish calendar to see when the 14th day of Nisan is after the Paschal Full Moon.\n\nAlternatively, I could look up the spring equinox date for this year and see if Passover starts around that time. But I think the more accurate method is using the Paschal Full Moon. So, I should probably look up the date of the Paschal Full Moon for this year and then add 14 days to find the start of Passover.\n\nI'm a bit confused about how to calculate the Paschal Full Moon's date without specific tools.", "T-Budget": "Okay, so I need to figure out when Passover started this year. I remember Passover is a Jewish holiday, but I'm not exactly sure about the exact dates. I think it's related to the story of the Exodus from Egypt, where the Israelites were freed from slavery. So, Passover must be around the time when they were in Egypt, but I'm not certain about the specific month and year.\n\nI know that Passover is celebrated in the spring, usually in March or April. But I'm not sure if it's always the same date every year. I think it might depend on the lunar calendar or something like that. Maybe it's based on the Paschal Full Moon? I've heard that term before, but I'm not exactly clear on how it works.\n\nWait, I think Passover is tied to the spring equinox. The equinox is when day and night are roughly equal in length, and that usually happens in March. So, Passover might start around that time. But I'm not sure if it's exactly on the equinox or a few days before or after. I think it's around March 29th or 30th, but I'm not certain.\n\nAlso, I remember hearing that Passover can fall on different dates each year because of the way the lunar calendar aligns with the solar calendar. So, sometimes it might be a week earlier or later. I'm not sure how that works exactly, but I think it's because the Jewish calen