r 110 years old. I think she's still alive, but I'm not sure about the exact age. I should check if her age is still recognized or if there have been any challenges in verifying her age.\n\nI also remember hearing about a man named Samiriyu Kep from the Gambia. I think he was around 115 years old, but I'm not sure if he's still alive. His age might be in question because of the challenges in verifying his age, especially considering his background and the circumstances of his life.\n\nSo, putting this together, the oldest verified living person would be the one whose age is still widely accepted and confirmed. If Jeanne Calment is still alive, she might be the oldest, but if she's deceased, then the next candidates would be Ethel Maud Emick or Samiriyu Kep. However, I'm a bit confused about whether her age is still verified or if there have been any disputes.\n\nI should also consider the sources of this information. Are there any organizations that maintain records of the oldest people? I think the Guinness World Records is a common source, but I'm not sure if they update their records frequently or if there are discrepancies. Maybe I should look up the latest records to confirm the ages and verify their status as living or deceased.\n\nAnother thing to consider is the methodology used to verify age. For example, in some cases, the age might be based on the person's date of birth and their current age, but if they were born in a different calendar system or had a different naming convention, that could affect their age verification. For instance, some cultures use different names for people who reach certain ages, which might make it harder to verify their exact age.\n\nI also wonder about the legal aspects. In some countries, there might be requirements for age verification, especially for benefits or pensions. If someone is over a certain age, they might need to provide proof of their age, which could affect whether their age is considered verified.\n\nSo, to sum